- op: create  # dc49
  at: '2026-11-17T22:57:15Z'  # dc49
  by: agent  # dc49
  actor: jgstern_agent  # dc49
  clock: 1  # dc49
  nonce: dc49  # dc49
  data:  # dc49
    kind: invariant  # dc49
    title: "A test file whose module runs changed import-time code through an import inside a function is not selected: the import graph does not follow function-level imports even when the function runs at import"  # dc49
    status: violated  # dc49
    priority: 1  # dc49
    parent:  # dc49
    tags:  # dc49
    - smart_test  # dc49
    - trunk_green  # dc49
    - ci_infrastructure  # dc49
    isbefore: []  # dc49
    duplicate_of: []  # dc49
    not_duplicate_of: []  # dc49
    pr_ref:  # dc49
    description: "FOUND 2026-11-17 22:58 UTC, while verifying the fix for INV-podom on the real pipeline.\n\nWHAT IS WRONG. For a change to import-time code, smart-test adds the test files whose imports reach the changed file (coverage-select import-closure; hypergumbo_core.import_closure). The import graph deliberately leaves out an import written INSIDE A FUNCTION of a source module (\"counting those put cli's whole closure under every test file of the real tree\"; the bed's known miss, test_reads_lazily). But a function can run while its module is imported, and then the import inside it runs at import too:\n\n  hypergumbo_lang_common/haskell.py:738   <module>  calls _derive_haskell_gated_names()\n  hypergumbo_lang_common/haskell.py:733   _derive_haskell_gated_names  imports and calls io_boundary.load_catalog\n  hypergumbo_lang_mainstream/py.py:942    <module>  calls _derive_external_constructor_types()  (py.py is loaded by jupyter.py:73)\n  hypergumbo_lang_mainstream/py.py:923    _derive_external_constructor_types  imports and calls io_boundary.load_catalog\n\nSo loading haskell.py or jupyter.py runs io_boundary.load_catalog, and neither file has an import edge to io_boundary.py in the graph.\n\nLIVE REPRO (real commands, live index, dev 1e39f0f3b, 2026-11-17 22:56 UTC), one statement inserted at the top of io_boundary.load_catalog:\n  scripts/coverage-select graph-walk --changed-files <io_boundary.py> --output W     -> 750 files, 556 test files\n  scripts/coverage-select import-closure --changed-files <io_boundary.py> --base HEAD -> 523 test files (the function is marked as run at import since PR #2240)\nOf the 9 test_haskell*.py / test_jupyter*.py files, 3 are in both answers and 6 are in NEITHER: test_haskell_equation_span.py, test_haskell_handle_argument_origin.py, test_haskell_zero_arg_actions.py, test_jupyter.py, test_jupyter_cyclomatic.py, test_jupyter_producer_stable_ids.py. Each loads a module whose import runs the edited function. They are not in the manifest for that change.\nNotebook: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/fast_smart_test_build_11172026/bar/import_time_functions.txt.\n\nTHE SAME GAP FOR A MODULE-BODY CHANGE of io_boundary.py: not checked directly, but the graph is the same graph.\n\nWHAT THE INDEX NOW KNOWS THAT CAN CLOSE IT. Since PR #2240 the index marks the functions a run saw execute under no test's context (at_import_block): _derive_haskell_gated_names and _derive_external_constructor_types are such functions. An import inside a MARKED function runs at import, and can be an edge of the import graph without bringing back the cost that got function-level imports excluded.\n\nNOT ESTABLISHED: whether a merged change ever lost a failing test this way."  # dc49
    fields:  # dc49
      statement: "A test file that loads a module whose import executes changed import-time code is in the selection, whatever the syntactic position of the import that carries the dependency."  # dc49
      root_cause: "import_closure counts an import as an edge only where it runs at definition (module level, class body, decorators, defaults). An import inside a function that the module calls while loading runs at import too, and is not counted."  # dc49
